/-!
# Reduced base change along a geometrically reduced algebra

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 034N, `lemma-geometrically-reduced-any-reduced-base-change`,
in `literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex`
(blueprint: lem-trf-geometrically-reduced-base-change).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

/-- Let `k` be a field and `A` a commutative `k`-algebra remaining reduced after every
field extension. For every reduced commutative `k`-algebra `R`, the ring `R ⊗[k] A` is
reduced. Neither algebra is required to be nonzero or finitely generated. The tensor ring
has its usual multiplication on pure tensors and unit `1 ⊗ₜ 1`.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 034N, `lemma-geometrically-reduced-any-reduced-base-change`
(blueprint: lem-trf-geometrically-reduced-base-change).

Proof plan:
1. Every `k`-module is flat (`lem-weil-pbc-module-over-field-flat`). Tensor exactness
   (`lem-weil-pbc-flat-tensor-exact`) therefore makes tensoring an injective linear map
   with `A` injective. For algebra inclusions these are also unital ring homomorphisms.
2. A finitely generated `k`-subalgebra `B` of `R` is reduced and Noetherian
   (`lem-wf-affine-noetherian-components`). Its total fraction ring is a finite product
   of its minimal-prime localizations, which are fields
   (`lem-weil-pbc-reduced-noetherian-total-ring-fractions`). The localization kernel
   criterion (`def-weil-pbc-module-localization`) gives an injection from `B` into this
   product. Each field has its induced `k`-algebra structure. If `B` is zero, its tensor
   product with `A` is zero, so handle that case directly.
3. Tensor product (`def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product`) commutes with this finite
   product: send `(x_i) ⊗ₜ a` to `(x_i ⊗ₜ a)_i`. Its inverse is the finite sum of
   the tensor maps of the coordinate insertions. Checking on pure tensors proves
   the inverse identities; the multiplication and unit formulas make this a ring
   isomorphism. No assertion about an infinite product is needed.
4. Apply the hypothesis to each field factor and swap the tensor factors. Each
   resulting ring is reduced, hence so is their finite product. The injection of
   step 1 now descends reducedness to `B ⊗[k] A`.
5. Write a nilpotent tensor in `R ⊗[k] A` as a finite sum of pure tensors, and choose
   `B` generated by their finitely many `R`-coordinates. Injectivity in step 1 lifts
   its nilpotence to `B ⊗[k] A`; step 4 makes it zero, so the original tensor is zero.
   Empty sums and zero algebras are included. Test fields are taken in a universe
   containing `k`, `A`, and `R`, so these field factors can always be lifted into it. -/
theorem isReduced_tensorProduct_of_forall_fieldExtension
    (k : Type u) (A : Type v) (R : Type w) [Field k] [CommRing A] [CommRing R]
    [Algebra k A] [Algebra k R]
    (hgeom : ∀ (L : Type (max u v w)) [Field L] [Algebra k L], IsReduced (A ⊗[k] L))
    [IsReduced R] : IsReduced (R ⊗[k] A) := by
  sorry
